-- title:
--   Bound [k₀:𝔽ₚ] for 𝔪-torsion in regular differentials
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic a prime $p$, and let $N\ge 1$ satisfy $N\ne 0$ in $K$. Let `Fam` be a commuting family of $K$-linear endomorphisms $T_\ell$ of $K(\!(q)\!)$ indexed by the primes (a family together with the assertion that any two of its members commute), subject to: for every prime $\ell\nmid N$, $T_\ell$ is the operator [`LaurentSeries.heckeT`](def/LaurentSeries_HeckeV.html#L39) $=U_\ell+\ell\,V_\ell$ of weight $2$, where $U_\ell f$ has $n$-th coefficient $f_{\ell n}$ and $V_\ell f$ has $n$-th coefficient $f_{n/\ell}$ if $\ell\mid n$ and $0$ otherwise; and for every prime $\ell\mid N$, $T_\ell=U_\ell$. Let $k_0$ be a finite field that is an algebra over $\mathbb{Z}/p$, let $\theta_0$ assign to each prime an element of $k_0$ such that the $\mathbb{Z}/p$-subalgebra of $k_0$ generated by the values of $\theta_0$ is all of $k_0$, and let $e:k_0\to K$ be a ring homomorphism. Consider $F=K(\tilde\jmath,\tilde\jmath_N)\subseteq K(\!(q)\!)$, the intermediate field generated over $K$ by `jqModC` and `jqNModC`, and inside $\Omega_{F/K}$ the submodule of those $\omega$ that at every place $v$ of $F/K$ can be written $f\cdot v.\mathrm{dCoord}$ with $f$ in the valuation subring of $v$. The theorem asserts that the intersection of this submodule with the preimage, under the $q$-expansion map on differentials `qExpansionDiffAlong` attached to the inclusion of $F$ into $K(\!(q)\!)$, of the annihilator part of `Fam` at $e\circ\theta_0$ — the intersection of the kernels of $Q(T)$ over all polynomials $Q$ in the variables indexed by the primes over $\mathbb{Z}/p$ whose image in $K$ vanishes at $e\circ\theta_0$ — is a finite-dimensional $K$-vector space of dimension at most $\dim_{\mathbb{Z}/p}k_0$.
--
--   This is the $q$-expansion principle bound on the $\mathfrak{m}$-torsion of the regular differentials on $X_0(N)$ in characteristic $p$, $\mathfrak{m}$ being the kernel of the system of Hecke eigenvalues $\theta_0$ with residue field $k_0$: equivalently, the $\mathfrak{m}$-torsion of weight-two cusp forms mod $p$ has $k_0$-dimension at most one. It feeds the statement bounding by one the rank of the corresponding Hecke torsion modulo the reduction map.
